wmyt - YouTube / PeerTube player dockapp for Window Maker
=========================================================

wmyt streams a YouTube or PeerTube video (or anything else yt-dlp can
resolve) inside a 64x64 Window Maker dockapp. The 16:9
video occupies a 56x32 band; the rows above and below optionally show a
scrolling title and the elapsed / total play time, drawn in the familiar
5x7 sea-green LED style of wmtop and friends.

    +----------------+
    |  SCROLLING TI  |   title marquee
    |################|
    |#### VIDEO #####|   56x32 video
    |################|
    | 01:23    10:34 |   elapsed / total
    +----------------+

Requirements
------------
  * libmpv (Debian: libmpv-dev), libX11, libXext
  * yt-dlp in $PATH - keep it current (e.g. `pipx install yt-dlp`), since
    YouTube changes break old versions quickly

Building
--------
    make
    sudo make install        # PREFIX=/usr/local by default

Usage
-----
    wmyt [options] [URL]

    -mode both|title|time|none   text rows shown at start (default both)
    -fg COLOR / -bg COLOR        text / background colour
    -mute, -loop
    -ytdl-format FMT             yt-dlp format (default: 144p H.264 + audio)

Drag the tile into the dock or clip. Without a URL, middle-click the tile
to play whatever URL is in the selection/clipboard. For safety only
http(s) URLs (or a bare youtube.com / youtu.be address) are taken from the
selection, so PeerTube links need their full https:// URL; local files can
still be given on the command line.

Mouse
-----
    left               pause / resume; with no video loaded, show/hide the
                       scrolling help text
    double left        open full size in mpv at the current position
    right              cycle text rows: both -> title -> time -> none
    shift + left       seek back 10s
    shift + right      seek forward 10s
    middle             play URL from PRIMARY / CLIPBOARD
    wheel              volume

How it works
------------
libmpv runs with vo=libmpv and the software render API, so mpv scales each
frame straight into a 56x32 buffer which is composited with the bevel and text
into a 64x64 framebuffer and pushed to the dockapp's icon window. Everything
runs in one poll() loop on the X connection and an mpv wakeup pipe.

